.TH S3 1
.SH NAME
s3 \- minimal Amazon S3 client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B s3
.RB [ -r
.IR region ]
.RB [ -e
.IR endpoint ]
.I credfile
.B ls
.I bucket
.RI [ prefix ]
.br
.B s3
.RB [ -r
.IR region ]
.RB [ -e
.IR endpoint ]
.I credfile
.B get
.I bucket key
.br
.B s3
.RB [ -r
.IR region ]
.RB [ -e
.IR endpoint ]
.I credfile
.B put
.I bucket key
.B <
.I file
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B S3
is a small Amazon S3 client written entirely in Limbo: it signs
requests with AWS Signature Version 4
.RI ( awsv4 (2))
and sends them over this tree's own TLS
.RI ( https (2)),
with no host tooling involved \(em the point being that AWS
interactions need none once the SSL work
.RI ( doc/ssl-modernization-plan.md )
shipped.
.PP
.B Ls
lists the objects in
.IR bucket
(optionally restricted to those under
.IR prefix ),
one key per line, via ListObjectsV2.
.B Get
fetches
.I key
from
.I bucket
and writes it to standard output.
.B Put
uploads standard input as
.I key
in
.IR bucket .
.PP
.B -r
sets the region (default
.BR us-east-1 );
.B -e
overrides the endpoint host (default the regional
.BR s3. \fIregion\fB .amazonaws.com ),
for an S3-compatible service. Requests use path-style addressing
(the bucket is in the URL path, not the hostname), so bucket names
containing dots \(em which break virtual-host TLS \(em still work.
.PP
.I Credfile
is one of the forms
.IR bsky (1)
and
.IR masto (1)
also accept: a file path (two lines: the access key id, then the
secret access key); the literal
.BR - ,
which asks
.IR factotum (4)
for a
.B "proto=pass service=aws"
key
.RB ( user= accesskeyid,
.BI !password= secret);
or a keyspec fragment (containing
.BR = )
that narrows that query. So S3 credentials can live in
.IR factotum (4)
and
.IR secstore (1)
alongside the social-client keys.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/s3.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR s3test (1),
.IR awsv4 (2),
.IR https (2),
.IR factotum (4)
.SH BUGS
Deliberately minimal: single-object
.BR ls / get / put ,
no multipart upload, no delete, no copy, no bucket operations, no
ACLs, no versioning, no presigned URLs. The whole object body is held
in memory, so this is unsuited to very large objects. XML responses
are scanned for the tags of interest
.RB ( <Key> ,
.BR <Code> ,
.BR <Message> )
rather than parsed \(em enough for S3's flat, well-formed
ListObjectsV2 and Error documents, not general XML. The
.B put
and
.B get
paths were exercised only against AWS's own signed error responses
(a bogus key draws
.BR InvalidAccessKeyId ),
since driving real object writes from a test would need real
credentials and a real bucket; see
.IR s3test (1).
